<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sigurd the Crusader</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1090–<a href="/years/1130">1130</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Sigurd Jorsalfar, Sigurd I of Norway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sigurd I, known as Sigurd the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> (Jorsalfar), was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1103 to 1130. Between 1107 and 1111 he led the Norwegian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a>, sailing with a large fleet by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, where he helped King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin I of Jerusalem</a> capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> in 1110. He was the first European king to go on crusade in person.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigurd-the-crusader.jpg" alt="Sigurd the Crusader" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sigurd_the_Crusader.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sigurd was one of three sons of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-barefoot">Magnus III of Norway</a>, all of them illegitimate and born to different mothers, the others being Øystein and Olaf. Before he was proclaimed king he was styled King of the Isles and Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>. In 1098 he accompanied his father on an expedition to Orkney, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a>, and was made Earl of Orkney, with the title King of the Isles after the overthrow of its king. A marriage was arranged between Sigurd and a daughter of the Irish ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muirchertach-ua-briain">Muirchertach Ua Briain</a>. When Magnus was ambushed and killed in Ulaid in 1103, the 14-year-old Sigurd returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, leaving his child bride behind.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The three brothers were proclaimed kings in 1103 and ruled jointly to avoid feuds. Olaf died in 1115 and Øystein in 1123, after which Sigurd ruled alone. Historians have regarded the period as a golden age of the medieval Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. In 1107 he led the Norwegian Crusade to support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, with about 5,000 men in some 60 ships, according to the sagas. He fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> islands, visited King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, and was welcomed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin I of Jerusalem</a>.</p><p>Sigurd joined Baldwin and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doge-of-venice">Doge of Venice</a>, Ordelafo Faliero, at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, which fell on 5 December 1110. He was given a splinter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a>, which he kept. He sailed on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where he gave his ships and many treasures to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a>, and many of his men stayed to serve in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a>. He returned overland, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and was back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1111.</p><p>In his reign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithe</a> was introduced, which strengthened the church, and he founded the diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavanger">Stavanger</a>. In 1123 he took part in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> to Småland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, whose inhabitants were reported to have returned to Old Norse gods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the kings' sagas Morkinskinna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a>, Sigurd showed signs of mental illness in his last years. He died in 1130 and was buried in Hallvard's church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>. He left no legitimate son. His illegitimate son Magnus became king and shared the throne uneasily with Harald Gille, and a struggle among claimants after Sigurd's death grew into a lengthy and devastating civil war. Scholars still debate the historical reliability of the sagas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sigurd the Crusader” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 4 December 1110 Baldwin I of Jerusalem and <b>Sigurd I of Norway</b> took it after a siege and made it the centre of the Lordship of Sidon; Saladin captured it in 1187, German crusaders restored it in 1197, the Ayyubids destroyed it in 1249 and the Mongols…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Sidon surrendered in December 1110 to Baldwin I, helped by the Norwegian king <b>Sigurd the Crusader</b> and his fleet.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-sidon">Lordship of Sidon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.93,10.68&amp;year=1116" aria-label="Norway on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sigurd the Crusader"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5455 4969h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5455 4969h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.93,10.68&amp;year=1116">What was here in 1116? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/magnus-barefoot"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/magnus-barefoot.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Magnus Barefoot</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; 1073–1103</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Methinks it is a more useful and suitable accomplishment for a chief to be expert at his bow; and I think you could scarcely draw my bow, even if you took your foot to help.&#8221;</p><footer>Words to his brother and co-king Eystein (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Sigurd the Crusader) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; from the two kings' contest of boasts over their ale, as Snorri Sturluson tells it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It appears to me much better for a chief who is to be the superior of other men, that he is conspicuous in a crowd, and strong and powerful in weapons above other men; easily seen, and easily known, where there are many together.&#8221;</p><footer>Words to his brother and co-king Eystein (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Sigurd the Crusader) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; from the two kings' contest of boasts over their ale, as Snorri Sturluson tells it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
